    <!-- How it works -->
    <section class="sec" id="how" aria-labelledby="h-how">
      <div class="doc">
        <h2 class="h2" id="h-how">From one person's note to every agent's answer.</h2>
        <ol class="steps wide">
          <li class="step"><h3>Write</h3><p>Anyone can write a context item. It's short: what happened, where and from when, and how you know. An agent can suggest one too, and it waits for the owner like anything else.</p></li>
          <li class="step"><h3>Review</h3><p>It lands in the Inbox of whoever owns that area. They see whether the claims match the source, and what agents would say before and after. Then they publish it, or send it back with a note.</p></li>
          <li class="step"><h3>Agents read</h3><p>An agent asks over MCP and gets back the items that apply, each with its source. The reply opens with one line saying what was found, like Checked or Not found.</p></li>
          <li class="step"><h3>Gaps come back</h3><p>If questions about one topic keep finding nothing, the owner gets a card about it. We count the topic, not the words. It's how people find out what to write next.</p></li>
        </ol>
        <div class="wide">
<div class="how-pics">
          <div class="how-pic">
            <div class="how-txt"><h3>A context item is short</h3><p>What happened, where and from when, the sentence copied from its source, who owns it and when they last checked. That's all an agent reads.</p></div>
            <figure class="shot"><div class="frame"><picture class="light"><source data-shot="item-light-phone" media="(max-width: 640px)" srcset={shot('item-light-phone')} width="780" height="1560"><img data-shot="item-light" alt="A context item on the demo: Online sales to Norway stopped on 4 August 2026, with the sentence copied from the customs notice, its domain, owners, check date and source link." src={shot('item-light')} width="951" height="752" loading="lazy"></picture><picture class="dark"><source data-shot="item-dark-phone" media="(max-width: 640px)" srcset={shot('item-dark-phone')} width="780" height="1560"><img data-shot="item-dark" alt="" src={shot('item-dark')} width="951" height="752" loading="lazy"></picture></div></figure>
          </div>
          <div class="how-pic flip">
            <div class="how-txt"><h3>Gaps come back as counts</h3><p>Observability shows what agents asked about, by topic and answer line, and which questions found nothing. It keeps counts, not the words.</p></div>
            <figure class="shot"><div class="frame"><picture class="light"><source data-shot="observability-light-phone" media="(max-width: 640px)" srcset={shot('observability-light-phone')} width="780" height="1560"><img data-shot="observability-light" alt="Observability on the demo: what agents asked this week, by topic and answer line, with the share of checked answers and the questions that found nothing. Counts only." src={shot('observability-light')} width="1024" height="800" loading="lazy"></picture><picture class="dark"><source data-shot="observability-dark-phone" media="(max-width: 640px)" srcset={shot('observability-dark-phone')} width="780" height="1560"><img data-shot="observability-dark" alt="" src={shot('observability-dark')} width="1024" height="800" loading="lazy"></picture></div></figure>
          </div>
        </div>
        </div>
        <p class="rule-note"><b>No model picks the context.</b> Plain rules match the question's metric, market and dates against what's written. Footnote doesn't store numbers either. Your semantic layer returns the figure, and the item points at it.</p>
        <p class="doclink"><span>In the docs:</span><a href={docs('write-context')}>Write context</a><a href={docs('review-a-suggestion')}>Review a suggestion</a></p>
      </div>
    </section>

